            <ArticleTitle>Psychological changes in morbidly obese patients after sleeve gastrectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>404-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ciresp.2013.11.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0009-739X(13)00417-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The aim of this study is to observe the psychological changes at one year postop in a group of patients undergoing laparoscopic vertical sleeve gastrectomy (GVL) and multidisciplinary follow- up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 46 patients with a BMI-35 or higher, who were selected for GVL, completed psychological testing. After GVL surgery, patients received psychological, nutritional, and medical attention during 12 months, and they retook the same tests.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Psychological tests showed an improvement on almost all scales tested, except perfectionism. The most significant change was in the benchmark for Eating Disorders with an improvement of 89% for bulimia (P&lt;.01), and 55% for body dissatisfaction (P&lt;.01) and ineffectiveness (P&lt;.01). In quality of life there was an improvement of 57% in the change in health status (P&lt;.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">During our study, a protocol involving GVL and multidisciplinary follow-ups seems proved to be an effective intervention for improving bulimic symptoms and quality of living. The results of these psychological changes are similar to Roux-en-Y Gastric bypass but different to vertical banded gastroplasty or adjustable gastric band, according to previous studies. However, long-term studies are necessary to confirm this trend.</AbstractText>
